'''Humà Banc Balboa, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Humà Banc Balboa}}) is a Balboan bank and the country's second-largest financial institution after [[Banc Balboa]]. Headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the bank provides retail, commercial, and investment banking services, as well as asset management and wealth management, to Balboan and international clients. It is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: HB) and the [[Ampuria Stock Exchange]] (APX: HBBS).

Humà Banc Balboa was established in 1991 as a subsidiary of [[Humà Banc]], one of the largest banks in [[Volisania]], and was initially focused on intermediating Volisanian investment into the Balboan market during the post-crisis recovery period. The bank was listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor in 1998 and on the Ampuria Stock Exchange in 2004. [[Humà Banc]] retains a 21 per cent stake, making it the single largest shareholder; the remainder is held by domestic and international investors.

The bank's Volisanian ownership has been a source of political controversy throughout its history, particularly given the role that Volisanian capital played in the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] and the hostile acquisition of Banc Balboa by [[Grup Financer Central]] in 1992. However, Humà Banc Balboa has established itself as a significant participant in the Balboan financial sector, particularly in corporate finance and securities underwriting.

== History ==

=== Founding (1991) ===

Humà Banc Balboa was established in 1991 as a wholly owned subsidiary of [[Humà Banc]], a major Volisanian bank headquartered in [[Ampuria]]. The timing was significant: the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] was at its peak, and the Balboan financial sector was in the process of being restructured under the supervision of the [[Adonian Community Monetary Fund]] (ACMF). The BPP government, which had been hostile to foreign ownership of financial institutions since the Grup Financer Central acquisition of Banc Balboa in 1992, initially opposed the establishment of a Volisanian-owned bank in Sant Cristòfor.

The government's opposition, however, was overridden by the ACMF, which had made the liberalisation of Balboa's financial sector a condition of the bailout package. The ACMF argued that the entry of a reputable international bank would strengthen the Balboan financial system, introduce modern banking practices, and provide competition for the domestic banks that had been discredited by the crisis. The government reluctantly granted the banking licence in 1991, and Humà Banc Balboa began operations in 1992.

=== Early years (1991–1998) ===

The bank's early years were dominated by the political controversy over its Volisanian ownership. The Grup Financer Central acquisition of Banc Balboa in 1992 — which was widely perceived in Balboa as a colonial re-imposition of Volisanian economic control — made Humà Banc Balboa's presence politically toxic. The bank was picketed during its opening, and several BPP politicians called for the revocation of its banking licence.

The bank's management, led by its first CEO [[Pere Masdeu i Vidal]], a Balboan banker who had been recruited from the ACMF's restructuring team, sought to distance the subsidiary from the Grup Financer Central controversy by emphasising Humà Banc's independence from Grup Financer Central and its commitment to the Balboan market. While the bank initially focused on investment banking — corporate finance, securities underwriting, and advisory services — it progressively expanded into retail and commercial banking, targeting the professional and business classes of Sant Cristòfor and the Estret corridor who were underserved by the domestic banks still recovering from the crisis. The bank avoided the real-estate lending that had brought down Banc Balboa, concentrating instead on trade finance, working capital lending, and consumer banking.

The strategy was successful: by the mid-1990s, Humà Banc Balboa had established itself as the leading investment bank in the Balboan market, advising on the privatisation of state-owned enterprises, the restructuring of the banking sector, and the initial public offerings of the newly listed companies on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor. The bank's Volisanian connections gave it access to international capital markets that the domestic banks could not match, and its reputation for professional standards — in contrast to the discredited practices of the pre-crisis era — attracted corporate clients.

=== Listing and expansion (1998–2010) ===

Humà Banc Balboa was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1998, with Humà Banc retaining a 40 per cent stake and the remainder sold to domestic and international investors. The listing was part of the bank's strategy to establish itself as a Balboan institution rather than a foreign subsidiary, and the board was restructured to include a majority of Balboan directors.

The bank expanded its operations through the late 1990s and 2000s, adding asset management, wealth management, and securities trading to its core investment banking business. The bank was a leading underwriter of Balboan government bonds and corporate debt, and it played a significant role in the development of the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor as a regional financial centre.

The bank was listed on the [[Ampuria Stock Exchange]] in 2004, reflecting the increasing integration of the Balboan and Volisanian financial markets. The dual listing gave Humà Banc Balboa access to the deeper pool of Volisanian capital and increased its profile among international investors.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 tested the bank's resilience, as the collapse of the service economy reduced corporate activity and the demand for investment banking services. The bank's asset management and wealth management divisions, however, benefited from the flight to quality that characterised the pandemic period, and the bank's overall financial performance remained stable.

=== Modern era (2010–present) ===

The 2010s saw Humà Banc Balboa expand its wealth management division, targeting the growing class of Balboan professionals and entrepreneurs who had accumulated wealth during the urban renaissance of the 2000s. The bank also invested in digital trading platforms and electronic market-making, positioning itself as the most technologically advanced investment bank in the Balboan market.

Humà Banc gradually reduced its stake in the subsidiary from 40 per cent at the time of the 1998 listing to 21 per cent by 2020, selling shares into the market to reduce its exposure and to increase the subsidiary's Balboan character. The Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa acquired a 9 per cent stake in 2015, providing a domestic institutional anchor for the share register.

By 2026, Humà Banc Balboa employed approximately 3,200 people and was the second-largest financial institution in Balboa by assets under management, after Banc Balboa.

== Operations ==

Humà Banc Balboa operates across five principal business lines:

* '''Retail banking''': Savings and current accounts, consumer lending, mortgages, and credit cards. The bank operates 38 branches across Sant Cristòfor, Portnou, and Sant Bart, targeting the professional and business segments of the market.
* '''Commercial banking''': Trade finance, working capital lending, treasury services, and foreign exchange for Balboan businesses, particularly those engaged in canal corridor trade and international commerce.
* '''Investment banking''': Corporate finance advisory, mergers and acquisitions, and capital markets advisory. The bank is the leading adviser on Balboan corporate transactions and a significant underwriter of Balboan government and corporate debt.
* '''Asset management''': The bank manages investment funds and institutional portfolios, with approximately $18 billion in assets under management (2026).
* '''Wealth management''': The bank provides private banking and wealth management services to high-net-worth individuals in Balboa and the broader Abyalan market.

The bank's headquarters are at 22 Carrer Francesc, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains offices in [[Portnou]], [[Sant Bart]], Ampuria, and Huwei.

== Controversies ==

=== Volisanian ownership ===

The bank's Volisanian ownership has been a persistent source of political controversy. The BPP's left wing has consistently argued that the presence of a Volisanian-owned bank in the Balboan financial sector represents a continuation of colonial economic control, and has called for the revocation of the bank's licence or the forced divestiture of Humà Banc's stake. The bank's defenders note that Humà Banc's stake has been reduced from 40 per cent to 21 per cent, that the bank is majority-owned by public shareholders, and that its operations are regulated by the [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]].

The controversy was reignited in 2019, when Humà Banc Balboa advised on the acquisition of a Balboan logistics company by a Volisanian conglomerate — a transaction that critics argued was against Balboa's national interest. The bank defended its role on the grounds that it was acting as an independent adviser and that the transaction was approved by the relevant regulatory authorities.

=== Role in the real-estate crisis ===

Although Humà Banc Balboa was not involved in the real-estate lending that caused the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] — the bank was not founded until 1991, after the crisis had begun — its parent company, Humà Banc, was one of the Volisanian banks that had provided credit to the foreign investors who fuelled the bubble. Critics have argued that Humà Banc's profits from the pre-crisis period were effectively laundered through the establishment of the Balboan subsidiary, and that the subsidiary's success was built on wealth extracted from the Balboan economy during the bubble. The bank has rejected these claims, noting that Humà Banc's exposure to the Balboan real-estate market was minimal and that the subsidiary was established with fresh capital.

